.SH "NAME"
udev.conf \- Configuration for device event managing daemon
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
/etc/udev/udev\&.conf
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBudevd\fR(8)
expects its main configuration file at
/etc/udev/udev\&.conf\&. It consists of a set of variables allowing the user to override default udev values\&. All empty lines or lines beginning with \*(Aq#\*(Aq are ignored\&. The following variables can be set:
.PP
\fIudev_log\fR
.RS 4
The log level\&. Valid values are the numerical syslog priorities or their textual representations:
\fBerr\fR,
\fBinfo\fR
and
\fBdebug\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIchildren_max\fR
.RS 4
An integer\&. The maximum number of events executed in parallel\&. When unspecified or 0 is specified, the maximum is determined based on the system resources:
MIN(2 \(mu CPUs + 16, MAX(RAM / 128 MiB, 10), 2048), where
CPUs
is the number of CPUs
\fBudevd\fR
may run on, according to its CPU affinity, and
RAM
the size of the physical memory, or the memory limit of the cgroup of PID 1 when it is smaller:
\fImemory\&.max\fR
with cgroup v2, or
\fImemory\&.limit_in_bytes\fR
in the hierarchy of the memory controller with cgroup v1\&.
.sp
This is the same as the
\fB\-\-children\-max=\fR
option of
\fBudevd\fR(8)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIexec_delay\fR
.RS 4
Delay the execution of
\fIRUN\fR
instructions by the given number of seconds, or by a time span with a unit, e\&.g\&.
500ms\&. This option might be useful when debugging system crashes during coldplug caused by loading non\-working kernel modules\&. 0 means no delay, and a delay that is not shorter than the event timeout is ignored\&.
.sp
This is the same as the
\fB\-\-exec\-delay=\fR
option of
\fBudevd\fR(8)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIevent_timeout\fR
.RS 4
The number of seconds to wait for events to finish, or a time span with a unit, e\&.g\&.
5min\&. After this time, the event will be terminated\&. The default is 180 seconds, which is also used when 0 or less than a millisecond is given\&.
.sp
This is the same as the
\fB\-\-event\-timeout=\fR
option of
\fBudevd\fR(8)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIhwdb_format\fR
.RS 4
The format of the compiled hardware database
hwdb\&.bin
created by
\fBudevadm hwdb \-\-update\fR\&. Valid values are
\fB1\fR
and
\fB2\fR, invalid values are ignored with a warning\&. Defaults to
\fB1\fR\&.
.sp
Format
\fB1\fR
is the compatible format, which can be read by all versions of libudev\&. When a device matches multiple entries of the hardware database which set the same property, it is undefined which of the values is used\&.
.sp
Format
\fB2\fR
additionally stores the origin (the source file and the line number) of every property, so that for the same property the value from a later file or a later line takes precedence\&. This format can only be read by libudev from this release or newer: older versions of libudev, including an old
\fBudevd\fR
which was not restarted after an upgrade, misread such a database\&.
.sp
When
\fBudevadm hwdb \-\-update\fR
is used with
\fB\-\-root=\fR, the value is taken from
udev\&.conf
below the specified root directory instead, and defaults to
\fB1\fR
if it is not set there\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIsas_legacy_path\fR
.RS 4
Takes a boolean argument (\fByes\fR,
\fBtrue\fR,
\fB1\fR
or
\fBno\fR,
\fBfalse\fR,
\fB0\fR)\&. Defaults to
\fBno\fR\&. Selects how the
\fBpath_id\fR
builtin composes the SAS part of
\fIID_PATH\fR, and thus the
/dev/disk/by\-path/
links, of devices behind a SAS port whose
num_phys
attribute is not
1\&.
.sp
By default only ports with two or more phys are treated as wide ports and named after the SAS address of the end device:
sas\-\fI0xADDRESS\fR\-lun\-\fIN\fR\&. Ports reporting zero phys, as done e\&.g\&. by the smartpqi driver, are named like single phy ports after the phy they are connected to:
sas\-phy\fIPHY\fR\-lun\-\fIN\fR, or
sas\-exp\fI0xADDRESS\fR\-phy\fIPHY\fR\-lun\-\fIN\fR
when connected through an expander\&. If
num_phys
cannot be parsed, no
\fIID_PATH\fR
is set\&.
.sp
If set to
\fByes\fR, the naming of older eudev versions is used: every port with a
num_phys
other than
1, including
0
and unparsable values, is treated as a wide port and named
sas\-\fI0xADDRESS\fR\-lun\-\fIN\fR\&. This keeps existing by\-path links of such controllers stable\&. The file is only read when
\fBudevd\fR
starts, and the links of already present devices are updated only when their events are processed again, e\&.g\&. by
\fBudevadm trigger\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fInet_altnames\fR
.RS 4
Takes a boolean argument\&. Defaults to
\fByes\fR\&. When set to
\fBno\fR,
\fIOPTIONS+="net_altnames"\fR
is ignored, i\&.e\&. no alternative names are assigned to network interfaces, which
80\-net\-altnames\&.rules
requests by default (see
\fBudev\fR(7))\&. An invalid or empty value is ignored, with a warning whenever the configuration is read: when
\fBudevd\fR
starts, and by
\fBudevadm test\fR\&.
\fBudevd\fR
only reads the file when it starts, and alternative names are only assigned when an interface is added\&.
.RE
.PP
\fInet_naming_scheme\fR
.RS 4
Selects the naming scheme used by the
\fBnet_id\fR
builtin to generate the predictable names of network interfaces:
eudev
for the names generated by the
\fBnet_id\fR
builtin of eudev 3\&.2\&.15 and older, one of the schemes of systemd, e\&.g\&.
v255, or
latest, the default\&. The schemes are described in
\fBudev\fR(7)\&. Setting it keeps these names stable when a new version adds a new scheme\&. It does not select the rules that choose the name an interface is renamed to, which are the same with all schemes, e\&.g\&. the name from the hwdb is preferred, and interfaces named by userspace or predictably by the kernel are not renamed, also with
eudev\&.
\fInet\&.naming_scheme=\fR
on the kernel command line and the
\fI$NET_NAMING_SCHEME\fR
environment variable of
\fBudevd\fR, unless its value starts with
:, take precedence (see
\fBudevd\fR(8))\&. Unknown names are ignored with a warning, and the next setting in that order is used\&. The file is only read when
\fBudevd\fR
starts, and interfaces are renamed only when they are added, so a change takes effect at the next boot; also rebuild the initrd if it starts
\fBudevd\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fItimeout_signal\fR
.RS 4
Specifies a signal that
\fBudevd\fR
will send on worker timeouts\&. Note that both workers and spawned processes will be killed using this signal\&. Defaults to
\fBSIGKILL\fR\&. This is the same as the
\fB\-\-timeout\-signal=\fR
option of
\fBudevd\fR(8)\&. An invalid value is ignored, with a warning when
\fBudevd\fR
starts\&.
.RE
.PP
In addition,
udevd
can be configured by command\-line options and the kernel command line (see
\fBudevd\fR(8))\&. The command\-line options take precedence over the settings in this file, the options on the kernel command line take precedence over both, and for the maximum number of workers,
\fBudevadm control \-\-children\-max=\fR
over all of them\&. For
\fIchildren_max\fR,
\fIexec_delay\fR
and
\fIevent_timeout\fR, 0 counts as not set, so e\&.g\&.
\fIudev\&.children_max=0\fR
on the kernel command line does not override
\fIchildren_max=4\fR
in this file\&. Invalid values of these settings are ignored, with a warning when
\fBudevd\fR
starts\&. The file is only read when
\fBudevd\fR
starts\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBudevd\fR(8),
\fBudev\fR(7),
\fBudevadm\fR(8)
